REGIONAL REGULATION OF SURAKARTA CITY

NUMBER 7 OF 2024

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

MAYOR OF SURAKARTA,

COPY

Considering:

a. that women are citizens who have human rights that must be protected, respected, maintained, and may not be ignored, reduced, or deprived by anyone, thus requiring guaranteed protection from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination and need to be empowered so that they can actualize their potential optimally;
b. that in order to realize the fulfillment of women's constitutional rights to be free from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination, as well as to improve the quality of life of women, they need to receive protection and empowerment from the Regional Government;
c. that the implementation of protection of women in the Region has not been able to be carried out optimally and is accompanied by the continued occurrence of acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women, thus requiring a comprehensive regulation to provide direction, foundation, and legal certainty;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 16 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large City Regions Within the Province of East Java, Central Java, West Java and the Special Region of Yogyakarta (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 45);
3. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
4. Law Number 12 of 2022 concerning the Criminal Act of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6792);
5. Law Number 11 of 2023 concerning Central Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 58 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6867);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SURAKARTA CITY

and

MAYOR OF SURAKARTA

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN.

Article 4

1. Every woman has the right:
a. to life;
b. to have a family and continue offspring;
c. to self-development;
d. to obtain justice;
e. to personal freedom;
f. to security;
g. to welfare; and
h. to participate in government.
2. The right to life as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a includes:
a. the right to sustain life and improve one's standard of living;
b. the right to obtain a sense of tranquility, security, peace, happiness, physical and spiritual welfare; and
c. the right to a good and healthy living environment.
3. The right to have a family and continue offspring as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, namely the right to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage in accordance with religion and the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. The right to self-development as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, includes:
a. the right to the fulfillment of basic needs to grow and develop properly;
b. the right to protection for personal development, to obtain education, educate oneself and improve the quality of life to become a human being who is faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous in accordance with human rights;
c. the right to develop and obtain benefits from science and technology, arts and culture in accordance with human dignity for the welfare of oneself, the nation, and humanity;
d. the right to communicate and obtain information necessary to develop oneself and one's social environment in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
e. the right to seek, obtain, own, store, process, and convey information using all types of available means in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
f. the right to fight for the right to self-development, both individually and collectively, to build society, nation, and state in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
g. the right to perform social work in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
h. the right to participate in the planning and implementation of government policies in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. the right to organize and form organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and
j. the right to organize education and teaching in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms.
5. The right to obtain justice as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, includes:
a. the right to receive equal treatment without discrimination;
b. the right to obtain justice;
c. the right to submit applications, complaints, and lawsuits, whether in criminal, civil, or administrative cases; and
d. the right to be tried through a free and impartial judicial process, in accordance with procedural law that guarantees an objective examination by an honest and fair judge to obtain a just and correct decision.
6. The right to personal freedom as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, includes:
a. the right not to be enslaved or subjugated, trafficked, and all acts with similar purposes;
b. the right to personal integrity, both spiritual and physical;
c. the right not to be the object of research without one's consent; and
d. the right to freely express and convey opinions with due regard to religious values, morality, order, public interest, and national integrity.
7. The right to security as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:
a. the right to protection of oneself, family, honor, dignity, and property rights;
b. the right to recognition before the law as a human person; and
c. the right to be free from threats to do or not do something that violates religious, social, and legal norms.
8. The right to welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) letter g, includes:
a. the right to have welfare, either alone or together; and
b. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with one's work that is comparable and commensurate with human dignity in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
9. The right to participate in government as referred to in paragraph (1) letter h, includes:
a. the right to be elected and to vote in general elections; regional head elections, and/or other political office elections based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret, honest and fair voting according to the applicable laws and regulations;
b. the right to have a career in government; and
c. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

