Article 6

1. The forms of Violence referred to in Article 5 include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic violence;
e. exploitation; and
f. GBOV.
2. Economic Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d includes:
a. taking full control over the victim's income, including salary, savings, or other financial assistance received by the victim;
b. restricting or prohibiting the victim from owning or using money, so that the victim is completely dependent on the perpetrator for financial needs;
c. prohibiting or preventing the victim from working or continuing education with the aim of limiting the victim's financial independence;
d. using the victim's assets or property for personal gain or damaging such assets as a form of punishment; and
e. making all financial decisions without involving the victim, so that the victim has no control over household finances.
3. Exploitation as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e at a minimum:
a. exploiting women's labor by providing inadequate wages, imposing excessive working hours, or not providing safe and healthy working conditions;
b. exploiting women for sexual purposes, by coercion, deception, or pressure. Including human trafficking for the purpose of prostitution or pornography production; and
c. exploiting women unfairly in economic transactions, such as providing loans with very high interest or manipulating the market for personal gain.
4. GBOV as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:
a. comments or messages that are insulting, demeaning, or belittling women;
b. messages or comments that threaten to commit physical or sexual violence against women;
c. disseminating the victim's personal information (such as address, telephone number, or other identity) without permission with the aim of intimidating or harming the victim;
d. extortion involving threats to disseminate intimate images or videos of the victim unless the victim meets the perpetrator's demands;
e. disseminating private photos or videos of an intimate nature without the consent of the woman in such content;
f. use of technology to stalk or monitor the victim continuously, often with the aim of frightening or controlling the victim; and
g. digitally altering images or videos of women for the purpose of insulting, humiliating, or sexually exploiting.

CHAPTER V

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 26

1. The Regional Government in its efforts to provide and administer handling services for the protection of women victims of violence, exploitation and acts of violence shall establish UPTD PPA.
2. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be under and responsible to the head of the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
3. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (2) shall have the duties to:
a. receive reports or outreach to Victims;
b. provide information about the rights of Victims;
c. facilitate the provision of health services;
d. facilitate the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitate the provision of psychosocial services, social rehabilitation, social empowerment, and social reintegration;
f. provide legal services;
g. identify economic empowerment needs;
h. identify temporary shelter needs for Victims and Families of Victims that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitate the needs of Victims with Disabilities;
j. coordinate and work together on the fulfillment of Victims' rights with other institutions; and
k. monitor the fulfillment of Victims' rights by law enforcement officials during the judicial process.
4. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be grouped into the following roles:
a. health;
b. psychology;
c. social-education;
d. law; and
e. economy.
5. Further provisions regarding the establishment, main duties and functions as well as management of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 5

1. Every woman victim of Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination has:
a. the right to handling;
b. the right of Victims to Protection; and
c. the right of Victims to recovery.
2. The right to handling as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a includes:
a. the right to information on all processes and results of Handling, Protection, and Recovery;
b. the right to obtain handling result documents;
c. the right to legal services;
d. the right to psychological strengthening;
e. the right to health services including examination, treatment, and medical care;
f. the right to services and facilities in accordance with the special needs of victims; and
g. the right to deletion of sexual content for cases of sexual violence using electronic media.
3. The right of Victims to Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b includes:
a. provision of information regarding rights and Protection facilities;
b. provision of access to information on the implementation of Protection;
c. protection from threats or violence by perpetrators and other parties as well as recurrence of violence;
d. protection of identity confidentiality;
e. protection from attitudes and behavior of law enforcement officials that demean Victims;
f. protection from loss of employment, job transfer, Education, or political access; and
g. protection of Victims and/or reporters from criminal prosecution or civil lawsuits for Criminal Acts of Sexual Violence that have been reported.
4. The right of Victims to recovery as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c includes:
a. medical rehabilitation;
b. mental and social rehabilitation;
c. social empowerment;
d. restitution and/or compensation;
e. social reintegration; and
f. receiving skills training for economic improvement for vulnerable women.

REGIONAL REGULATION OF SURAKARTA CITY

NUMBER 7 OF 2024

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

MAYOR OF SURAKARTA,

COPY

Considering:

a. that women are citizens who have human rights that must be protected, respected, maintained, and may not be ignored, reduced, or deprived by anyone, thus requiring guaranteed protection from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination and need to be empowered so that they can actualize their potential optimally;
b. that in order to realize the fulfillment of women's constitutional rights to be free from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination, as well as to improve the quality of life of women, they need to receive protection and empowerment from the Regional Government;
c. that the implementation of protection of women in the Region has not been able to be carried out optimally and is accompanied by the continued occurrence of acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women, thus requiring a comprehensive regulation to provide direction, foundation, and legal certainty;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b, and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Protection of Women;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 16 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Large City Regions Within the Province of East Java, Central Java, West Java and the Special Region of Yogyakarta (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1950 Number 45);
3. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
4. Law Number 12 of 2022 concerning the Criminal Act of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6792);
5. Law Number 11 of 2023 concerning Central Java Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 58 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6867);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF SURAKARTA CITY

and

MAYOR OF SURAKARTA

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING PROTECTION OF WOMEN.

